
🧠 HashMap — Internal Working (Deep Dive)
🧩 1. Introduction

HashMap in Java is a part of java.util package and implements the Map interface.
It stores data as key–value pairs, allowing fast retrieval based on keys.
It’s non-synchronized, allows one null key and multiple null values, and is not ordered.

🧱 2. Internal Data Structure

Internally, a HashMap uses an array of Nodes (also called buckets).

transient Node<K,V>[] table;


Each Node (also known as an entry) looks like this:

static class Node<K,V> implements Map.Entry<K,V> {
    final int hash;
    final K key;
    V value;
    Node<K,V> next;  // for chaining (linked list)
}


So, each index of the array can hold a linked list (or a red-black tree in JDK 8+).

⚙️ 3. How HashMap Works Internally

When you put a key-value pair:

map.put("John", 25);

Step-by-step flow:
1️⃣ Compute hash

hashCode() of the key is called:

int hash = key.hashCode();


Then Java applies a hashing function to reduce collisions:

hash = hash ^ (hash >>> 16);


(This mixes high bits and low bits for better distribution.)

2️⃣ Calculate bucket index
index = (n - 1) & hash;


Where n is the length of the table (usually a power of 2).
This ensures the index stays within array bounds.

3️⃣ Check if bucket empty

If empty, create a new Node and place it there.

If not empty, check:

If key already exists → replace value.

If key different → add a new node to the linked list (collision handling).

⚔️ 4. Collision Handling

Two keys can have the same index (hash collision).
Example: “FB” and “Ea” have the same hash code!

Before JDK 8:
All collided entries formed a linked list at that bucket.

After JDK 8:
If the number of nodes in a bucket > 8,
→ The linked list is converted to a Red-Black Tree for faster O(log n) lookup.

🪄 5. Rehashing and Load Factor

HashMap expands when the number of elements exceeds:

threshold = capacity × loadFactor


Default:

capacity = 16
loadFactor = 0.75
threshold = 12


When exceeded → it rehashes, i.e., doubles the capacity and repositions all nodes.

🔁 6. get() Method Working
map.get("John");


1️⃣ Compute hash and index → same as put()
2️⃣ Go to that index (bucket).
3️⃣ Traverse list/tree:

If key found → return value.

Else → return null.

