1️⃣ How does HashMap handle collisions?

👉 Collisions occur when two keys map to the same bucket index.

In JDK 7 and below, collisions were handled using a Linked List inside each bucket.

In JDK 8 and later, once the number of entries in a bucket exceeds 8, the linked list is converted to a Red-Black Tree, reducing lookup time from O(n) to O(log n).

2️⃣ Difference between HashMap and Hashtable?
Feature	HashMap	Hashtable
Synchronization	Non-synchronized (not thread-safe)	Synchronized (thread-safe)
Nulls allowed?	1 null key, many null values	No null keys/values allowed
Performance	Faster (no locking)	Slower (locks entire map)
Introduced in	Java 1.2 (Collection Framework)	Java 1.0 (legacy)
Modern alternative	ConcurrentHashMap	Deprecated for new code
3️⃣ What is the significance of hashCode() and equals()?

hashCode() decides which bucket an entry belongs to.

equals() decides if two keys are actually the same (to update or replace value).

✅ If two keys are equal, their hashCode() must be equal.
If not implemented properly, you can get duplicate or missing entries.

4️⃣ What is load factor and when does resizing occur?

Load Factor = how full the HashMap can get before resizing.

Default load factor = 0.75.

When size > capacity × loadFactor, HashMap rehashes:

Capacity doubles.

All entries are rehashed and redistributed into new buckets.

5️⃣ How does HashMap maintain performance in Java 8+?

Introduced balanced tree (Red-Black Tree) in heavily collided buckets (> 8 nodes).

Introduced hash spreading function hash ^ (hash >>> 16) for better distribution.

Result: avoids long linked lists → consistent O(1) average time even under collisions.

6️⃣ Why are power-of-2 table sizes used in HashMap?

It makes index calculation super fast:

index = (n - 1) & hash;


Bitwise AND is faster than modulus (%) and distributes entries more uniformly.

Example: if capacity = 16, the mask (16-1) = 15 (1111b) → keeps only lower 4 bits of hash → efficient indexing.

7️⃣ What happens if two keys have same hash but are not equal?

They go into the same bucket (collision).

During lookup, HashMap compares their equals() method to find the correct key.

If equals returns false, they’re treated as different entries in the same bucket list/tree.